-When the `dryrun` argument is set to `FALSE`, a `list` of `SummarizedExperiment` and/or `TreeSummarizedExperiment` objects is returned. The `rownames` argument determines the type of `rownames` to use for `relative_abundance` resources: either `"long"` (the default), `"short"` (the lowest available taxonomic name, usually species), or `"NCBI"` (NCBI Taxonomy ID). These are not the same vocabulary, which matters if you merge with profiles generated elsewhere. `"long"` is the unmodified MetaPhlAn clade string, and matches an external MetaPhlAn profile exactly when that profile came from the same database (`mpa_v30_CHOCOPhlAn_201901` here). `"short"` and `"NCBI"` are resolved against NCBI when the packaged taxonomy is built, so they are a snapshot of NCBI at that point rather than a live lookup, and they can disagree with the MetaPhlAn names: MetaPhlAn3 reports `Bacteroides_dorei`, while `"short"` gives `Phocaeicola_dorei`. Those taxa fail to match on a merge by name, with no error raised, which affects about 7% of returned rows. Join on `"long"` names, or on NCBI Taxonomy ID using the integer `rowData()` species column, which is stable across genus reclassification (the genus-level identifier is not). That integer column is only present when the resource was requested with `rownames = "NCBI"`: the examples above use `"short"`, whose `rowData()` holds character names. Do not join on the row names from `rownames = "NCBI"`: they are labels and can carry a rank prefix such as `species:`, so they will not match bare identifiers from another source. When a single resource is requested, a single element `list` is returned.
